UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 2: Dance elements and technique', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning. Slow movement can feel careful, heavy or sad because the audience has time to notice each part. Quick movement can feel urgent, playful or startled because the action arrives and vanishes. The same reach is a different idea if it fills four counts or only one. Dancers choose duration on purpose, then say what that length communicates. You can clap or write the counts if you would rather not move.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA dancer steps toward a closed door. Version A uses four slow steps. Version B uses four quick steps in the same direction. What different meaning does each duration suggest, and which clue shows that time, not a new step, made the change?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSlow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency. The clue is that direction and step shape stayed the same, so duration is the element that changed the meaning. Naming \"slow\" or \"quick\" without a meaning only describes the clock. A count chart is enough if you do not step the phrase.\n\nWATCH FOR\nMoving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score. Duration is a meaning choice, not a ranking. A slow walk can be the stronger choice when the idea is waiting. Quicker is not automatically more skilled, and slower is not automatically weaker.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite two timings for a new head turn, with a different meaning for the slow version and the quick version. Use counts, and do not reuse the window reach or the door walk. Trying the timings is optional, gentle, and only with an adult nearby.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Dance","learning_intention":"Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning. Slow movement can feel careful, heavy or sad because the audience has time to notice each part. Quick movement can feel urgent, playful or startled because the action arrives and vanishes. The same reach is a different idea if it fills four counts or only one. Dancers choose duration on purpose, then say what that length communicates. You can clap or write the counts if you would rather not move.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DA4-PER-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write two timings for a new head turn, with a different meaning for the slow version and the quick version. Use counts, and do not reuse the window reach or the door walk. Trying the timings is optional, gentle, and only Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using duration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score.. Correction: Slow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score. Duration is a meaning choice, not a ranking. A slow walk can be the stronger choice when the idea is waiting. Quicker is not automatically more skilled, and slower is not automatically weaker. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating duration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with duration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about duration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for duration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for duration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Slow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score"],"question":"A dancer steps toward a closed door. Version A uses four slow steps. Version B uses four quick steps in the same direction. What different meaning does each duration suggest, and which clue shows that time, not a new step, made the change?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for duration; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score","Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning","Slow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency","Careful use of slow movement with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named slow movement, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score","Name slow movement only, without linking it to duration","Apply duration to new material and name the slow movement used"],"question":"Which response best applies duration with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on duration. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Slow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for duration"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score","Name slow movement only, with no linked example","Write two timings for a new head turn, with a different meaning for the slow version and the quick version"],"question":"Which independent response best applies duration to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about duration with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating duration on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using duration on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for duration. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with duration, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score.. Correction: Slow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-dance-y9-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Dance 7–10 (2023) outcome DA4-PER-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Movement is optional, pain-free, and adult-supervised. Written plans are acceptable evidence.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise duration.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use duration with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify slow movement and explain why it matters.","I can practise quick movement independently on a new prompt.","I can notice time choice needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm. Ari keeps the arm path identical and performs, or writes, the same reach in one quick count, which reads as sudden alarm. Because the shape did not change, the audience can tell that duration carried the new meaning. Ari''s chart says: counts 1-4 slow reach means searching; count 1 quick reach means alarm.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA dancer steps toward a closed door. Version A uses four slow steps. Version B uses four quick steps in the same direction. What different meaning does each duration suggest, and which clue shows that time, not a new step, made the change?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSlow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency. The clue is that direction and step shape stayed the same, so duration is the element that changed the meaning. Naming \"slow\" or \"quick\" without a meaning only describes the clock. A count chart is enough if you do not step the phrase.","explain":"Duration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning. Slow movement can feel careful, heavy or sad because the audience has time to notice each part. Quick movement can feel urgent, playful or startled because the action arrives and vanishes. The same reach is a different idea if it fills four counts or only one. Dancers choose duration on purpose, then say what that length communicates. You can clap or write the counts if you would rather not move.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score. Duration is a meaning choice, not a ranking. A slow walk can be the stronger choice when the idea is waiting. Quicker is not automatically more skilled, and slower is not automatically weaker. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast treating speed as a score with using duration to change what a movement means.","caption":"Duration is a meaning choice. Faster is not automatically a better dance idea.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Speed as a score</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Faster always means better</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Time as meaning</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Duration changes the idea</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: A reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm. Ari keeps the arm path  Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: A reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm. Ari keeps the arm path  Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm. Ari keeps the arm path identical and performs, or writes, the same reach in one quick count, which reads as sudden alarm. Because the shape did not change, the audience can tell that duration carried the new meaning. Ari''s chart says: counts 1-4 slow reach means searching; count 1 quick reach means alarm. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels show the same kind of reach lasting four slow counts as a search, or one quick count as an alarm.","caption":"Both durations can be correct. Changing the length changes the meaning of the same action.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Slow duration</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Four counts, careful search</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Quick duration</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">One count, sudden alarm</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMoving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score. Duration is a meaning choice, not a ranking. A slow walk can be the stronger choice when the idea is waiting. Quicker is not automatically more skilled, and slower is not automatically weaker. Here is a more helpful example: A reach toward a window that fills four slow counts reads as careful searching, not as an alarm. Ari keeps the arm path identical and performs, or writes, the same reach in one quick count, which reads as sudden alarm. Because the shape did not change, the audience can tell that duration carried the new meaning. Ari''s chart says: counts 1-4 slow reach means searching; count 1 quick reach means alarm.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSlow steps toward the door suggest caution, while the same quick steps suggest urgency. The clue is that direction and step shape stayed the same, so duration is the element that changed the meaning. Naming \"slow\" or \"quick\" without a meaning only describes the clock. A count chart is enough if you do not step the phrase.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Moving faster always makes a dance better, because speed is treated only as a fitness score. Duration is a meaning choice, not a ranking. A slow walk can be the stronger choice when the idea is waiting. Quicker is not automatically more skilled, and slower is not automatically weaker. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about duration?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDuration is how long a movement lasts, and a slow or quick choice changes the meaning. Slow movement can feel careful, heavy or sad because the audience has time to notice each part. Quick movement can feel urgent, playful or startled because the action arrives and vanishes. The same reach is a different idea if it fills four counts or only one. Dancers choose duration on purpose, then say what that length communicates. You can clap or write the counts if you would rather not move. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 2: Dance elements and technique","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Dance elements and technique","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"